dict(
slot="Security", label="SECURITY NEWS", cats=[17],
slug="optimjack-optimizely-malware-178-websites-ledger-bbc-crypto",
title="'Optimjack' Malware Hit 178 Brands Including Ledger, BBC and Zoom via Optimizely",
excerpt="Attackers abused a flaw in Optimizely's signup flow to inject code that swapped crypto wallet addresses and skimmed card details on websites of 178 organisations on Monday.",
kw="Optimjack malware, Optimizely breach, crypto clipboard hijacker, Ledger website malware, card skimming attack",
seo_title="Optimjack Malware Hits 178 Brands via Optimizely Flaw",
desc="Attackers used an Optimizely flaw to inject crypto-stealing and card-skimming code into sites of 178 firms including Ledger, BBC and Zoom. How to stay safe.",
tags=["Optimizely","Optimjack","Malware","Supply Chain Attack","Ledger","Crypto Security","Card Skimming","Cybersecurity","JavaScript","Report URI","Data Breach","Security News"],
html=f"""
<p>Websites run by some of the world's best-known brands served malware designed to steal visitors' cryptocurrency and card details on Monday, November 16, after attackers abused a flaw at website optimisation company Optimizely.</p>

<p>The campaign, dubbed "Optimjack" by researcher Amirul Iman, affected website scripts belonging to 178 organisations, including hardware wallet maker Ledger, OANDA / Coinpass, trading platforms IG, Interactive Investor and TMS Brokers, as well as IKEA, Salesforce, Zoom, the BBC and CNN, <a href="https://cointelegraph.com/news/hackers-inject-malware-into-178-major-websites" {A}>Cointelegraph reported</a>, citing an investigation by Report URI.</p>

<h2>How the attack worked</h2>

<p>Optimizely lets businesses run experiments and custom code on their websites through JavaScript snippets. An Optimizely spokesperson confirmed that an unauthorised party exploited a vulnerability in its Experimentation account signup flow to add malicious JavaScript to some customer projects. "We have blocked access to the vulnerable path and deployed a fix," the company said, adding that it removed the injected code, ended active sessions, revoked tokens and notified affected customers.</p>

<p>Once loaded, the code could replace cryptocurrency wallet addresses with attacker-controlled ones, including addresses copied to a visitor's clipboard. It also watched for card numbers, expiry dates and security codes entered into website forms, though it could not read payment fields inside third-party payment providers' secure frames, according to StepSecurity. Visitors on iPhones and iPads faced an extra threat from code linked to iOS exploit kits, Report URI chief executive Scott Helme said.</p>

<p>The BBC was among the first sites seen serving the injection at 4:21 a.m. UTC. By 9:52 p.m. UTC, only one of 188 infected snippets still carried malicious code, according to Report URI. Researchers have not yet established how the attackers first gained access.</p>

<h2>Losses so far appear small</h2>

<p>Crypto addresses linked to the malware received around $7,100 on Monday, Cointelegraph found. Galaxy Digital's Alex Thorn estimated victims sent only about $2,300 in total, saying most receipts appeared to be the attacker's own funds. Report URI could not determine how many card details reached the attacker's server. Salesforce said it was investigating and had disabled Optimizely scripts on affected sites as a precaution.</p>

<p>The incident is the latest in a run of attacks on shared vendors that serve many companies at once, following recent third-party breaches at Expedia and France's Maileva. In October, prediction market Polymarket briefly suspended trading after a third-party provider was compromised, Cointelegraph noted.</p>

<h2>What users and businesses should do</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Check every pasted address.</strong> Compare the first and last several characters of any wallet address before you send. On hardware wallets, confirm the address on the device screen.</li>
<li><strong>Watch your cards.</strong> If you entered card details on an affected site on November 16, review statements and consider asking your bank for a new card.</li>
<li><strong>Update Apple devices</strong> to the latest iOS or iPadOS version.</li>
<li><strong>For website owners:</strong> audit third-party scripts, use a content security policy and subresource integrity where possible, and monitor for unexpected changes to tag managers and testing tools.</li>
</ul>

<p>For African fintechs and exchanges that rely on the same global marketing and analytics tools, the attack is a reminder that a vendor's weakness can quickly become your customers' loss.</p>
"""),

