dict(
slot="AI", label="ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE", cats=[2, 642],
slug="mashatile-africa-tech-festival-africa-must-build-ai",
title="Africa Must Build AI, Not Just Consume It, Mashatile Tells Africa Tech Festival",
excerpt="Opening the 29th Africa Tech Festival in Cape Town, South Africa's Deputy President Paul Mashatile set out five priorities and said AI could help build a single African digital market.",
kw="Africa Tech Festival 2026, Paul Mashatile AI, African AI strategy, single African digital market, AfCFTA digital",
seo_title="Africa Must Build AI, Mashatile Tells Africa Tech Festival",
desc="South Africa's Deputy President Paul Mashatile opened Africa Tech Festival 2026 urging Africa to build and export AI, and set out five digital priorities.",
tags=["Artificial Intelligence","Africa Tech Festival","Paul Mashatile","South Africa","AfCFTA","Digital Economy","Data Centres","Cloud Computing","Digital Skills","Cape Town","AI Policy","Tech Africa"],
html=f"""
<p><strong>CAPE TOWN</strong> — Africa must become a builder and exporter of artificial intelligence, not merely a consumer of tools developed elsewhere, South African Deputy President Paul Mashatile said as he opened the 29th Africa Tech Festival on Tuesday, November 17.</p>

<p>The continent cannot rely on technologies "trained on data that does not reflect our realities and designed without our priorities in mind," Mashatile said, <a href="https://techtrendske.co.ke/2026/11/17/mashatile-africa-tech-festival-ai-keynote/" {A}>TechTrendsKE reported</a>. "We must be innovators, builders and exporters of the technologies that will define the future." He added: "For perhaps the first time in modern economic history, Africa has an opportunity not to catch up, but to leapfrog." He delivered the address remotely from Pretoria, <a href="https://www.timeslive.co.za/news/sci-tech/2026-11-17-ai-could-be-key-to-single-african-digital-market-mashatile/" {A}>TimesLIVE reported</a>.</p>

<h2>Five priorities</h2>

<p>Mashatile set out five priorities for Africa's digital agenda, <a href="https://itweb.africa/article/mashatile-outlines-africas-digital-priorities/PmxVEMKE5QnvQY85" {A}>ITWeb reported</a>:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> more data centres, fibre and 5G, and secure, sovereign cloud. He said cloud adoption alone could unlock more than R185 billion (about $11 billion) for South Africa.</li>
<li><strong>People:</strong> skills in AI, cyber security, data science and entrepreneurship. "Africa's most valuable resource in the digital era is its human talent, not its natural resources," he said.</li>
<li><strong>Innovation:</strong> moving "from merely celebrating innovation to actively commercialising it."</li>
<li><strong>Trust:</strong> stronger cyber security, data protection and AI frameworks that reflect Africa's "values, languages and development priorities."</li>
<li><strong>Integration:</strong> using the African Continental Free Trade Area to build a single digital market with interoperable payments and harmonised rules.</li>
</ul>

<p>AI could help drive the continent toward a single digital market with seamless, secure payments, creating more room for trade and development, Mashatile said, according to TimesLIVE.</p>

<h2>A warning on a two-speed Africa</h2>

<p>He warned that millions of Africans still lack affordable broadband, skills shortages persist and connectivity remains too costly for many households. "If left unaddressed, these challenges risk creating a two-speed Africa: one connected, innovative and prosperous, and another excluded from the opportunities of the digital age," he said.</p>

<p>Referencing South Africa's G20 digital-economy priorities, he argued that Africa must act with agency in shaping AI, ITWeb reported. "Africa will not compete in the age of artificial intelligence through imitation. We will compete by solving our own challenges, harnessing our own talent, building our own capabilities and creating solutions that the world will want to use," he said, according to TechTrendsKE.</p>

<h2>Big Tech in the room</h2>

<p>The three-day festival at the Cape Town International Convention Centre runs until November 19 and covers telecoms, data centres, AI, cyber security, startups and digital transformation. Speakers include executives from OpenAI, Meta, Amazon, MTN, Vodacom, Orange and Cassava Technologies. Its programme includes a keynote on what would make Africa an AI producer rather than a consumer, with Andela chief executive Carrol Chang and Lelapa AI co-founder Jade Abbott, <a href="https://techcentral.co.za/one-festival-nine-stages-what-to-expect-at-africa-tech-festival/288217/" {A}>TechCentral reported</a>. Another session brings together investors from British International Investment, the IFC, Verdant Capital and African Infrastructure Investment Managers to discuss financing the continent's AI and data centre needs.</p>

<p>The speech came as President Cyril Ramaphosa leads a delegation of nearly 300 people in Silicon Valley, where he said he would meet OpenAI, Anthropic, Microsoft and Google.</p>

<h2>Why it matters</h2>

<p>For African startups and fintechs, the message is that governments want local AI models, data centres and payment links that work across borders. The test will be whether policy moves as fast as the rhetoric: funding for compute, clear data rules and real progress on AfCFTA's digital protocol.</p>
"""),

